page("compact-grill-camping-guide","Camping With a Compact Charcoal Grill","Car camping, RV and campsite cooking on the Slot Grill Compact: fire rules, packing list, campsite setup, camp menu, mini fire pit use, and leave-no-trace cleanup.",IMG["c13"],f"""
<p>Shared campground grills can be anything from rusted to used as a trash burner. Bringing your own small grill means you know what's been cooked on it, you can set it up where it suits your site, and you can cook whether or not the site has a fire ring grate. The Slot Grill Compact is sized for one to three people, weighs about 14 lb, and packs flat to about 1" thick, so it slides into a camp box, trunk, kayak hatch or RV storage. It assembles by hand with tab and slot construction: no tools, no hinges, no loose hardware to lose in the dirt.</p>
<blockquote>"Exactly what I was looking for. Have already used twice while camping."<cite>Neil Cavazza, Compact owner</cite></blockquote>
<h2>Before you go</h2><ul><li><b>Check the rules.</b> Campgrounds, parks and public lands set their own fire rules and may issue burn bans. Some allow charcoal only in fire rings or designated grills; some ban all fires in dry periods. Check the campground or land manager before you pack fuel.</li><li><b>Season at home.</b> Raw carbon steel cooks and stores best seasoned. See <a href="/how-to-season-a-compact-grill.html">how to season</a>.</li><li><b>Pack charcoal dry.</b> A dry bag or lidded bin keeps dew and rain off. Damp charcoal is miserable to light.</li><li><b>Prep food at home.</b> Marinate, chop and portion before you leave; label bags in cook order.</li><li><b>Plan meals for the grill size.</b> 146 sq in suits meals for one to three; cook in waves for more.</li></ul>
<h2>Camp cooking packing list</h2><ul><li>Slot Grill Compact (and a <a href="{GRID}">griddle</a> for breakfast)</li><li>Chimney starter, charcoal, newspaper or natural starters, two lighters</li><li>Long tongs, spatula, heat-safe gloves</li><li><a href="{THERMO}">Instant-read thermometer</a></li><li>Metal can with a lid for cold ash</li><li>Cooler with ice; separate cooler or zone for raw meat</li><li>Cutting board, knife, foil, foil pans, paper towels</li><li>Water, biodegradable soap, a small scrub pad, trash bags</li><li>Oil for the grates and a rag or paper towels</li><li>Headlamp for after-dark cleanup</li></ul>
<h2>Setting up at camp</h2><ol><li>Pick bare soil, gravel, sand or a pad, away from tents, awnings, vehicles, overhanging branches and dry grass.</li><li>Clear leaves and debris from around the grill.</li><li>Assemble: hold an end piece with SLOT GRILL facing out, slide in the two-tab side panel, add the second end piece, then seat the remaining side panel. Check it's level.</li><li>Light a chimney and build a <a href="/two-zone-fire-on-a-small-grill.html">two-zone fire</a>.</li><li>Keep water nearby.</li></ol>
<h2>A three-day camp menu that fits the Compact</h2><div class="tbl"><table><tr><th></th><th>Breakfast</th><th>Dinner</th></tr><tr><td>Day 1</td><td>From home</td><td><a href="/steak-fajitas-compact-grill.html">Steak fajitas</a> (marinated at home)</td></tr><tr><td>Day 2</td><td><a href="/campfire-breakfast-griddle.html">Bacon, eggs and potatoes</a></td><td><a href="/chicken-thighs-small-charcoal-grill.html">Chicken thighs</a> and <a href="/grilled-vegetables-compact-grill.html">vegetables</a></td></tr><tr><td>Day 3</td><td>Breakfast tacos with leftovers</td><td><a href="/sausage-and-peppers-compact-grill.html">Sausage and peppers</a>, then <a href="/smores-and-grilled-desserts-compact-grill.html">s'mores</a></td></tr></table></div><p>Cook the most perishable food first, and keep the cooler cold.</p>
<h2>Mini fire pit after dinner</h2><p>Where fires are allowed, the same grill works as a small fire pit for marshmallows after dinner. Add a little cooking wood or charcoal to the remaining coals, and keep it small. Never leave it unattended and put it fully out before bed.</p>
<h2>Wildlife and food storage</h2><p>Grill smells attract animals. Clean the grates after cooking, pack food and trash away according to the campground's rules, and don't leave grease or scraps on the ground.</p>
<h2>Honest tradeoffs</h2><p>Thick steel is heavier than a flimsy folding grill. At about 14 lb the Compact is for car, truck, boat and RV camping, not backpacking. And carbon steel needs to be kept dry and lightly oiled after trips or it will surface-rust. In exchange, you get a grill strong enough to support cast iron and built to last.</p>
<h2>Leave no trace cleanup</h2><ol><li>Let coals burn down completely. Never leave a fire unattended.</li><li>Make sure ash is cold to the touch; if allowed, drown and stir.</li><li>Put ash in a metal can with a lid and follow the park's disposal rules.</li><li>Scrape the grates, wipe them with a little oil, and pack the grill flat once fully cool.</li><li>Pack out all trash and food scraps.</li></ol>
<h2>When you get home</h2><p>Wash if needed, dry completely, re-oil the cooking surfaces and store dry. See <a href="/how-to-clean-and-store-a-compact-grill.html">clean and store</a> and <a href="/rust-prevention-carbon-steel-grill.html">rust prevention</a>.</p>
{SAFETY}
""","trips",[("Can I bring my own charcoal grill to a campground?","Many campgrounds allow it, but rules vary. Some only allow charcoal in fire rings or designated grills, and burn bans can prohibit fires. Check with the campground first."),("Is the Slot Grill Compact light enough for backpacking?","No. At about 14 lb it's built for car, truck, boat and RV camping, not backpacking."),("Can the Compact be used as a fire pit?","Where fires are allowed, owners use it as a small fire pit for marshmallows after dinner. Follow local rules and never leave it unattended."),("How do I keep the grill from rusting on camping trips?","Season it at home, keep it dry, wipe the cooking surfaces with oil after use, and dry it fully before storing.")])

page("compact-grill-tailgate-guide","Tailgating With a Compact Charcoal Grill","How to run a tailgate on the Slot Grill Compact: lot rules, setup next to the truck, packing list, menu timing, holding food, and leaving the lot safely.",IMG["both16"],f"""
<p>A tailgate grill has three jobs: fit in the truck with the coolers, set up fast in a crowded lot, and feed people without babysitting. The Slot Grill Compact packs about 1" thick and weighs about 14 lb, so it rides behind a seat or under a cooler. It assembles in 30–60 seconds by hand, with no tools or hinges, and it's 12-gauge carbon steel, so it doesn't wobble when people crowd around it.</p>
<blockquote>"We got this for my husband for Father's Day and he loved it. Commented on how well it was built and is making plans to use it on the tailgate at the jobsites."<cite>Kimi Hines, Compact owner</cite></blockquote>
<h2>Know the lot rules first</h2><p>Stadiums, venues and parking lots set their own rules. Many require grills to be a set distance from vehicles, limit open flames, require hot coals to be disposed of in designated bins, or set tailgating hours. Check before game day.</p>
<h2>Where to set it up</h2><p>Not on the tailgate itself. Put it on pavement or gravel next to the truck, on a stable, level, non-combustible surface, away from vehicles and foot traffic. The 2⅞" air gap under the coal bed helps minimize radiant heat to the surface below, but it's still a charcoal fire. Keep it out of the walkway so nobody kicks it.</p>
<h2>Packing list</h2><ul><li>Slot Grill Compact (and a <a href="{GRID}">griddle plate</a> for smash burgers or breakfast)</li><li>Chimney starter, charcoal, lighter, newspaper</li><li>Long tongs, spatula, heat-safe gloves</li><li><a href="{THERMO}">Instant-read thermometer</a></li><li>A metal can with a lid for cold ash</li><li>Foil pans for resting and holding meat, foil, trash bags, paper towels</li><li>Coolers: one for drinks, one for raw meat on ice</li><li>Folding table for prep (keep it away from the grill)</li><li>Water and hand soap or wipes</li></ul>
<h2>Menu timing for a small grill</h2><p>Plan in waves. The 146 sq in surface does sausages or brats first (they hold well in a foil pan), then burgers, then anything quick like peppers and onions on the griddle. Two-zone coals keep the waves going without the fire getting away from you.</p><div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Wave</th><th>Food</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td><a href="/sausage-and-peppers-compact-grill.html">Brats or sausages</a></td><td>Forgiving, hold well</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><a href="/smash-burgers-on-a-griddle.html">Smash burgers</a></td><td>Fast, crowd favorite</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><a href="/chicken-wings-small-charcoal-grill.html">Wings</a> or <a href="/grilled-shrimp-compact-grill.html">shrimp</a></td><td>Snackable</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Peppers, onions, corn</td><td>Uses the fading fire</td></tr></table></div><p>For bigger groups see <a href="/cooking-for-a-crowd-small-grill.html">cooking for a crowd</a>.</p>
<h2>Game-day timeline</h2><ol><li>Arrive, park, set up the table and grill.</li><li>Light the chimney right away; coals take time.</li><li>Pour, build zones, preheat grates.</li><li>Cook in waves; light a second chimney if you're feeding a lot of people.</li><li>Stop cooking well before you need to leave so the fire can burn down.</li></ol>
<h2>Keeping food safe in the lot</h2><p>Keep raw meat in a cooler at 40°F or below. Don't leave cooked food out more than 2 hours (1 hour above 90°F). Hold cooked food hot in a covered foil pan at the cool end of the grill.</p>
<h2>Leaving the lot</h2><p>Never pack hot steel or ash into a vehicle. Let the coals burn down, dispose of them where the venue allows (many have hot-coal bins), and stack the grill once it's cool. Old gloves help, steel and soot go together. A bag or old box keeps soot off the truck bed.</p>
<h2>Job site lunches</h2><p>Owners also take the Compact to job sites for lunch. The same rules apply: a safe, non-combustible spot away from materials and vehicles, permission from the site, and a fully cold grill before it goes back in the truck.</p><blockquote>"Good quality, perfect for on the go grilling, and great for job sites for the blue collar guys out there."<cite>Verified Compact owner</cite></blockquote>
{SAFETY}
""","trips",[("Can I put the grill on my tailgate?","No. Set it on pavement or gravel next to the vehicle on a stable, level, non-combustible surface, away from vehicles and foot traffic."),("How fast does the Slot Grill Compact set up?","About 30 to 60 seconds by hand, with tab and slot assembly and no tools or hinges."),("What's the best tailgate food for a small grill?","Foods that hold well and cook fast: sausages, smash burgers, wings and griddle vegetables, cooked in waves."),("How do I get rid of hot coals at a stadium?","Use the venue's hot-coal bins if provided, or let them burn down and cool completely. Never pack hot ash or a hot grill into a vehicle.")])
